                "type": "practice",
                "title": "Map the Institutional Family Tree",
                "content": "Build a quick institutional lineage to lock in the chronology this lesson establishes, since it matters for everything that follows.\n\n**Fill in the blanks yourself before checking:**\n\n1. Socrates dies in Athens (year: ___). He founded no formal school and left no writings.\n2. Plato, his student, founds the Academy roughly ___ years later, in a grove sacred to ___.\n3. In 367 BCE, a 17-year-old named ___ arrives at the Academy from the city of ___.\n4. That student remains at the Academy for ___ years before eventually founding his own rival school, the Lyceum (covered in a later lesson).\n5. The Academy itself, remarkably, keeps operating for centuries after Plato's death, finally closing in 529 CE under the emperor ___.\n\n**Answers:** (1) 399 BCE; (2) c. 387 BCE, roughly 12 years later, grove of Academus; (3) Aristotle, from Stagira; (4) 20 years; (5) Justinian I.\n\nNotice the structure: one institution (the Academy) directly bridges Socrates's oral, unwritten teaching to Aristotle's systematic treatises — the entire trajectory of this book runs through that one physical grove outside Athens.",
